"""IT 2026-11-26 (IT28): new wording for N02 (ADV-294, card F04) and for SCI's ADV-282 increments 4 to 6 (cards A01, A02, B01, E01, F01, F04, H02, I01, K01, L01)
plus the DR-L01-003 addendum. Data only. Hypothesis level; reference genotype class; nothing here is medical advice.
Entries are (id, card, field, new_text). 'field' is 'note', 'measured_result_note' or 'endpoint' of the assertion with that id; F04 uses the p4 field path.
Card text may not carry a dose, route or schedule token (check_cards.py), so doses are described in words or left to the cited source."""

NEW = []
def e(i, card, field, text): NEW.append(dict(id=i, card=card, field=field, new=text))

e("N02", "F04_nmdar_intervention_class", "p4_fields.functional_evidence_available",
 "In the neuron-specific (Synapsin-Cre) knockout neocortical slice, d-APV abolishes spontaneous bursting, which recovers on washout, and suppresses a delayed evoked response at the three highest stimulus strengths (supplementary figure S3C, six slices from three animals, unpaired tests without correction); spontaneous excitatory current amplitude is about six percent larger (24.7 against 23.3 picoamperes) than in wild-type-plus-heterozygous controls at -70 mV, a shift of the pooled event distribution by Kolmogorov-Smirnov test with no per-cell test reported (inferred AMPAR-weighted, not pharmacologically isolated); spontaneous inhibitory current amplitude is about halved and its frequency reduced; the resting potential of pyramidal neurons is depolarised against wild type. No pharmacologically isolated NMDAR or AMPAR current and no receptor subunit protein or surface level has been found measured in any WWOX system (search of held sources, 2026-11-26); Wwox-knockout mouse hippocampus and hippocampal neural stem cells show lower Grin2c transcript, and the neural stem cell RNA-seq also lists lower Grin2b, not reported for hippocampus (Hussain 2019, PMID 30290271), and neuron-specific (Synapsin-Cre) knockout neocortical layer II/III pyramidal neurons show larger miniature excitatory currents at -70 mV, not pharmacologically isolated, with unchanged decay (Breton 2021, PMID 34634460). No subunit-selective, pore-blocking, glycine-site or AMPAR drug was found applied to any Wwox system.")

e("DR-A01-007", "A01_pba_class", "measured_result_note",
 "Mutant total protein about equal to untreated control, no significance mark (three experiments; bar reading), at the same applied level as the phenylbutyrate class; the text lists only the phenylbutyrate class and two osmolytes as stabilising. On wild type the same comparator gave a scattered, unmarked value near 1.95. The comparator (butyric acid in ChEMBL) was more potent than the phenylbutyrate class on HDAC3 and HDAC8 in one ChEMBL document; on HDAC1 and HDAC2 the held table gives butyric acid two values (16 and 300 micromolar for HDAC1; 12 and 400 micromolar for HDAC2) that bracket the phenylbutyrate values (64 micromolar for the acid, 162 micromolar for the sodium salt), and which document carries which value is not resolvable from the held aggregate rows, so that premise is document-dependent. In the held ChEMBL table its HDAC6 value is only a lower bound, above 2 millimolar, a bound that lies below the 5 millimolar applied, so HDAC6 inhibition at that level is neither shown nor excluded; no HDAC engagement was measured in this paper, and no pan-HDAC or HDAC6-selective arm exists in it. A measured null in a cell that is not comparable.")

e("DR-B01-002", "B01_amlexanox", "measured_result_note",
 "MEASURED NO PHENOTYPIC RESCUE with amlexanox: no improvement of mitomycin C survival (FANCA and FANCF lines), of diepoxybutane chromosome aberrations (FANCA line only) or of micronuclei (FANCC primary fibroblasts), at one amlexanox concentration, whereas the comparator ataluren improved all three; FANCA mRNA was not rescued. Full-length protein was induced in one arm (FANCA, from under 5 to about 9 percent of control; ataluren about 11 percent) and not in FANCF; the authors treat that induction as read-through. Amlexanox also lowered p53 (about 40 percent, FANCA line only, not FANCF) and phospho-STAT2 (about 50 percent, FANCA line), and it is these two falls that the authors attribute mainly, as a hypothesis with no direct TBK1 readout, to downstream TBK1/IKKe or NF-kB inhibition rather than read-through. The endpoint encoded here is the phenotypic rescue only, so the protein induction is stated in this note and not in the direction code. Encoded NO_CHANGE for the phenotypic endpoint.")

e("DR-W2-004", "F01_glutamate_receptor_node", "measured_result_note",
 "Memantine at the two higher of its three doses shortened the latency to all four seizure stages (stage 4 about 26 and 25 minutes against about 37 in controls, bar readings, eight per group by the analysis-of-variance degrees of freedom); the lowest dose shortened stages 1 and 2 only, although the text says every dose shortened each stage. Perampanel delayed the onset of stages 3 and 4 but did not prevent seizures. No in-study measure of receptor engagement. The reading carries unresolved internal inconsistencies noted in that record. A safety-direction datum for a non-genetic model, not WWOX.")

e("DR-H02-036", "H02_gabaat_inactivator_myelin_exposure", "note",
 "GABA-B engagement (baclofen) enhanced precursor differentiation, myelin protein and remyelination (purified rat precursor cultures, cerebellar slices after induced demyelination, adult mouse spinal cord after induced demyelination); in the purified precursor cultures the GABA-A agonist muscimol did not reproduce the effects (the abstract says none of them were mediated by it); in the dorsal-root-ganglion coculture the abstract reports only GABA itself (more myelin segments and myelin basic protein), without separating receptor classes. The muscimol test therefore differs from the GABA-B result, and no opposite sign is reported in the abstracts. Signalling studies, not drug exposures of a developing brain.")

e("DR-I01-014", "I01_pdk_inhibition_dca_premise_seizure_glycaemia", "endpoint",
 "average glycolysis with and without DCA, patient against control cells (lactate production was also measured)")
e("DR-I01-014", "I01_pdk_inhibition_dca_premise_seizure_glycaemia", "note",
 "DCA shifted flux in all cells, controls included, away from anaplerotic-related pathways (a 40 percent decrease) toward the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex, and decreased modelled average glycolysis by 29 percent in patient cells (one of six patient lines, with an unusual splicing mutation, excluded from the average) with no significant effect in the control cells of five healthy subjects; untreated patient cells (excluding the same splicing-mutation line) had higher glucose consumption and lactate production than controls. Lactate production and NTP/NDP did not change in either cell type, so the DECREASE code is glycolysis only; no genotype-by-DCA interaction test is reported. It is a precedent for a glycolysis-lowering responder phenotype. A genotype-by-inhibitor interaction on glucose-derived acetyl-CoA flux is the repository's proposed discriminating experiment, not a result of this abstract.")

# ---- ADD sentences (SCI section 4 and held-apart rows 3.2)
e("DA-L01-005", "L01_nrf2_axis", "measured_result_note",
 "ROS higher in late-passage Wwox-null fibroblasts than in wild-type and heterozygous ones (one DHE histogram per genotype shown, no quantification or test), no difference at early passage; at passage one a microarray (one column per genotype, heterozygous against null, not wild type; no n or test stated) shows redox-homeostasis transcripts, Nfe2l2 and Nqo1 among them, higher in nulls. NAC given continuously from cell isolation (prevention, not reversal) prevents the senescence escape; no ROS measurement under NAC was found. Non-neural; the authors leave an initial antioxidant response as an open question.")

e("DR-L01-003", "L01_nrf2_axis", "measured_result_note",
 "Different molecule. 'acute Nrf2 target engagement was not measured'; more broadly the authors state that Keap1 modification, Nrf2 nuclear translocation, ARE activation and Nrf2 target proteins were not measured at all, so superoxide dismutase and catalase activity after PTZ is the only antioxidant-enzyme readout, and it is not shown to be Nrf2-dependent; brain drug level not measured; one dose. Favourable in part: lower PTZ maximum severity and longer latency to the late stages (incidence difference not significant), and after status epilepticus lower spontaneous seizure burden and higher neuronal density in CA1 only (no difference in CA3; three animals per group in the histology; the abstract states CA1 and CA3, which the Results do not support); twenty-four hours after PTZ whole-brain cytokine mRNA and malondialdehyde were lower and superoxide dismutase and catalase activity higher (nine per arm), whereas cytokine mRNA differences were not significant at 10 weeks. MIXED-to-favourable, adult acquired epilepsy. Not transferable to omaveloxolone.")

e("DR-A02-010", "A02_proteasome_inhibitor_class", "endpoint",
 "serum total homocysteine and liver CBS activity under one week of low-rate carfilzomib")
e("DR-A02-010", "A02_proteasome_inhibitor_class", "measured_result_note",
 "No significant change in homocysteine or liver activity in the Results (the Discussion calls the activity change slight); a very slight rise in protein in some animals and no Hsp70 induction. The one-week pump schedule delivered about the same total amount as the 24-hour arm that rescued the regulatory-domain allele (DR-A02-004), at about one seventh of the rate; that 24-hour arm also showed no Hsp70 induction, so its absence here does not show absent engagement, and proteasome activity was not measured at this schedule. Tolerability is asserted by the authors only in general terms (low doses less toxic), not specifically for this arm; no weight, blood-count or clinical data are shown and group size is not stated. The allele rescued at the higher rate was therefore not rescued at the lower rate of delivery (PMID 37319242, Results 'Low dose/long-duration studies', last paragraph).")

e("DR-A02-006", "A02_proteasome_inhibitor_class", "measured_result_note",
 "Neither inhibitor restored significant activity ('a very small amount' with bortezomib), although proteasome inhibition was confirmed in this allele and CBS protein rose. The same allele gained activity with bortezomib in a human kidney-line model (DR-A02-008; that gain is measured from an essentially zero baseline, with signs of cell damage), so the allele does not fix the outcome: expression context and readout differ. A measured null in this system. The HEK paper's own summary of the mouse work says bortezomib partially rescued I278T and T191M, which is more generous than the mouse paper's wording; the mouse paper's wording is used here.")

e("DR-A02-007", "A02_proteasome_inhibitor_class", "measured_result_note",
 "With better-tolerated but not toxicity-free exposure the activity gain was not significant (the authors cite limited assay sensitivity at very low activity); a white-cell and lymphocyte reduction was still present and one animal was moribund. Homocysteine fell by about half (mean 345 to 179 micromolar, significant linear trend, compared with the starting value only, with no vehicle-injection arm; the authors allow handling as an explanation) yet stayed far above normal, with no significant activity gain; one of eight treated animals had the largest response and was also the one with the greatest weight loss and lowest white-cell counts. Adding the heat-shock co-inducer bimoclomol changed neither homocysteine, activity nor heat-shock induction. The authors conclude that proteasome inhibitors are probably not feasible as monotherapy at non-toxic exposure.")

e("DR-P9-009", "F04_nmdar_intervention_class", "measured_result_note",
 "Adults (18 or over, median age 63) with epilepsy and cognitive impairment, memantine added by patient choice for up to 24 weeks (nine of the memantine group discontinued), not randomised: increased seizure frequency (a rise of more than 25 percent in the Methods, 25 percent or more in the abstract) in 7 of 101 on memantine against 5 of 110 without it, no significant difference, four of the seven attributed to incidental antiseizure-drug non-adherence; baseline imbalances were in cognition, mood, age at onset and epilepsy duration, not in baseline seizure frequency; no drug level. Adult safety signal reassuring but non-randomised; it bears on the warning, not on children, not on a developmental epilepsy and not on benefit. The full text is held; targeted passages were read on 2026-11-26.")

e("DR-E01-002", "E01_isr_modulators", "measured_result_note",
 "Ratio 1.04 (95 percent credible interval 0.83 to 1.32) over a 24-week placebo-controlled period: no evidence of slowing, though the interval does not exclude a modest effect. Target engagement and proof of mechanism are cited from the earlier phase 1B trial, and the abstract reports none for this trial's participants. Adults with ALS; says nothing about a developmental encephalopathy.")

e("DR-E01-003", "E01_isr_modulators", "measured_result_note",
 "In ten-month-old 5xFAD mice over 23 days, with a donepezil comparison arm: better recognition memory and less microglial activation and tau; amyloid burden not reduced; p-eIF2 alpha lower; the abstract calls the compound an integrated-stress-response inhibitor and does not name its molecular target, and only recognition memory is reported as improved among the behavioural tests run. The donepezil results are not reported in the abstract. Mouse, adult, one dose, one study; the authors call the effects limited.")

e("DR-H02-008", "H02_gabaat_inactivator_myelin_exposure", "note",
 "CPP-115 only; vigabatrin itself was not tested in this paper. The reduction of spasms was only transient and the highest dose was lethal. The abstract states that spasm cessation was not accelerated and that neurodevelopmental behaviour and visuospatial learning were not altered; brain histology is not mentioned in the abstract. The held full-text page scores model-induced hippocampal injury (an extent score on stained sections of 23 brains from the vehicle and the two lower-dose groups, 91 percent scoring 3 to 3.5), equally distributed across those groups, and reports no myelin or white-matter histology.")

e("DR-H02-023", "H02_gabaat_inactivator_myelin_exposure", "note",
 "No microvacuolation or sheath splitting attributable to the drug was found, and no demyelination, in the 62 examined people (compared with ten untreated cases); two post-mortem and three operative specimens showed no vacuolation, and their overlap with the 62 is not stated. Abstract level: ages and exposure windows are not stated in the abstracts; the PubMed record of PMID 1940119 carries child, adolescent, adult and older headings and no infant heading (indexing, not abstract text). A negative in people not shown to include infants does not exclude the infant lesion the label records.")

e("DR-H02-024", "H02_gabaat_inactivator_myelin_exposure", "note",
 "Mild toxicity for myelinated fibres from vigabatrin and from GABA with no clear difference between them, although vigabatrin seemed slightly more toxic (the two were not applied at equal molar levels); degenerating myelinated fibres and astrocytic gliosis in both; no patent intramyelinic oedema and no primary demyelination were seen on electron microscopy, and the changes involved axons as well as myelin sheaths, so the row is not a vacuolation finding (its target is the in-vitro fibre endpoint). Preliminary data.")

e("DR-H02-030", "H02_gabaat_inactivator_myelin_exposure", "note",
 "No statistically significant difference between exposed and naive subjects among children aged 2 to 16 and adults (668 re-reviewed subjects); in infants of the same study the prevalence was higher with vigabatrin (22 against 4 percent; 205 infants with infantile spasms, a different indication from the complex partial seizures of the older arms), so age band and indication are confounded; the result for the older group is pooled over children and adults, and the infant prevalence comes from a population whose size the abstract does not give. Retrospective blinded MRI re-review of a prespecified T2 or FLAIR hyperintensity. The age-band contrast within one study is what the row carries. A non-significant difference is not proof of no difference.")

e("DR-I01-006", "I01_pdk_inhibition_dca_premise_seizure_glycaemia", "note",
 "A population in which lactate was persistently or intermittently raised, with a defined defect (pyruvate dehydrogenase deficiency in 11, respiratory chain in 25, mitochondrial DNA in 7 of 43), aged 0.9 to 19 years; the drug blunted the postprandial lactate rise and still did not change the clinical outcomes measured over six months. Heterogeneous and short; the abstract reports no PDH-only subgroup, so a null in this design does not exclude a benefit in a defined genotype.")

e("DR-I01-001", "I01_pdk_inhibition_dca_premise_seizure_glycaemia", "note",
 "PDH activity was 65 percent lower in chronic-stage pilocarpine mice than in non-epileptic mice and the drug partly alleviated this (significance not stated). In epileptic mice Ser300 phosphorylation was 52 percent higher and PDH protein 37 percent lower than in non-epileptic mice, and neither changed significantly with the drug. Two daily doses were tested, but the abstract does not say which was used for these assays and does not name the tissue. Total PDH protein fell 23 percent in non-epileptic mice on treatment, which the authors offer as a possible reason for the slight pro-convulsant signal.")

e("DR-I01-002", "I01_pdk_inhibition_dca_premise_seizure_glycaemia", "note",
 "Chronic treatment (timing relative to the test not stated) did not increase flurothyl latency; two doses were tested and the abstract does not say whether the null held at both, and gives no effect size; in the same paper the drug was slightly pro-convulsant in the 6 Hz test. PDH engagement is reported for the chronic-stage pilocarpine mice, not for these animals. One laboratory.")

e("DR-I01-004", "I01_pdk_inhibition_dca_premise_seizure_glycaemia", "note",
 "A primary seizure-endpoint test of a PDK inhibitor in a model in which PDH activity is documented to be reduced; second-hit latency was shorter in epileptic than in non-epileptic mice and DCA did not restore it; PDH activity was reported as partially alleviated (significance not stated) while Ser300 phosphorylation and PDH protein were not significantly changed, so engagement at the second-hit test is unclear; the abstract does not say whether the same animals were assayed. Abstract level; the full text is not held.")

e("DA-K01-007", "K01_hif_axis_glycolytic_shift", "measured_result_note",
 "A COUNTER-OBSERVATION to the organoid rows: no consistent elevation of HIF-axis or glycolysis transcripts across four contrasts; Hif1a within -0.21 to +0.06 log2, Hk2 flat (-0.02 to +0.37), Epas1 lower (-0.21 to -0.59, animal-separated in 3 of 4), Slc2a1, Ldha and Pgk1 lower in the group mean in all four contrasts (animal values overlap in some: Slc2a1 in the first hippocampal and the cortical contrast, Ldha and Pgk1 in cortex), Ndrg1 and P4ha1 lower in all four, Gfap higher. Log2 of group-mean summed-transcript TPM with a pseudocount of 0.01; two to four animals per arm; Wwox itself falls about 7- to 16-fold in the three hippocampal contrasts and only about 3-fold, with overlapping values, in cortex. Caveats: bulk tissue and TPM; the animals come from lines with severe epilepsy and death at 3 to 4 weeks; background differences between control groups; no protein; gliosis and hypomyelination change composition. It does not side with the organoid: a species or in-vivo-versus-organoid disagreement is the largest unresolved fact of this axis. Exploratory, post hoc (IT10 P2.1 disclosure).")

e("DR-P9-010", "F04_nmdar_intervention_class", "measured_result_note",
 "The only controlled paediatric exposure in this label (two studies in children aged 6 to 12 with autism spectrum disorders, one parallel placebo-controlled with 54 against 53 and one responder-enriched randomised withdrawal) failed to demonstrate efficacy: a null in a different disorder. The label calls the paediatric safety profile generally consistent with adults, states that safety and effectiveness in paediatric patients are not established and that the drug was not studied under 6 or over 12 years, and reports neuronal lesions in juvenile rats at the higher doses when dosing began on postnatal day 14, and neuronal degeneration at a lower dose, with behavioural toxicity at still lower doses, when dosing began on postnatal day 7. One extended-release capsule label held (one of 75 openFDA records; the others were not read).")

e("DR-P9-010", "F04_nmdar_intervention_class", "source",
 "openFDA drug label JSON for memantine (extended-release capsules), effective 2026-09-01; the held file is dated 2026-11-17 and 2026-11-18, and the earlier line said 'retrieved 2026-10-29' and no pull of that date was found among the held files by a file-name search on 2026-11-26 (NOT WWOX EVIDENCE)")
